/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  convDryer01_sugar_curve  --  CONTINUOUS CONVECTIVE DRYING ON THE
                               CHARACTERISTIC DRYING CURVE.

  solidDryer01's feed -- 3423 kg/h dry sucrose at X_in = 0.20 kg/kg, and
  800 kmol/h of air at 420 K with 2 mol% water -- split in two halves, each
  sent through the SAME continuous dryer (300 m2 of gas-solid contact,
  k_Y = 0.05 kg/(m2 s), X_c = 0.12 kg/kg, the characteristic curve
  f = Phi^1.5), one with the air flowing WITH the solid, one AGAINST it.
  (src/unitOperations/heatTransfer/ConvectiveDryer.H)

  Along the contact area the air COOLS and HUMIDIFIES as it dries the
  solid, so the wet bulb, the constant rate and the local equilibrium
  moisture all move -- what the batch tray (dryer02) cannot show.

                         co-current      counter-current
    X out  [kg/kg]       0.02342         0.00468
    water removed [kg/s] 0.0839          0.0929
    air out [K]          359.7           353.2
    Y out  [kg/kg dry]   0.0393          0.0421
    X_c crossed at       6.4 % of A      12.4 % of A
    floor: X_eq of the air as fed = 0.00110 kg/kg

  Counter-current dries five times further on the same area, because the
  driest solid meets the freshest (hottest, driest) air, where X_eq is
  lowest and the falling-rate driving force survives; co-current ends where
  the used-up air meets the driest solid.  That is the textbook result, and
  here it is computed, not asserted.

  THE FIRST LAW, on the package's own formation surface: each dryer
  publishes energyResidual_kW (1e-7 and 5e-10 kW), and the plant energy
  report closes at 1e-7 kW.  The hot air DECLARES `phase gas;` -- without
  it the stream carries vf = 0, the report prices the air as a liquid and
  the plant is off by 380 kW; the dryer now refuses that by name.

  HYPOTHESES (announced on every run): the solid sits at the LOCAL wet bulb
  in both periods -- it leaves at ~316 K, while a real solid warms toward
  the air in the falling period (not modelled); Lewis = 1; one k_Y over the
  whole area; no axial mixing; no re-wetting; adiabatic; bound moisture
  priced as liquid water.  X_c, k_Y and the curve are HYPOTHETICAL teaching
  values, not measured on this sugar.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        splitSolid;
        type        splitter;
        in          wetSolid;
        outputs     ( wetSolidCo  wetSolidCounter );
        operation   { fractions ( 0.5 0.5 ); }
    }
    {
        name        splitAir;
        type        splitter;
        in          hotAir;
        outputs     ( airCo  airCounter );
        operation   { fractions ( 0.5 0.5 ); }
    }
    {
        name        co;
        type        convectiveDryer;
        inputs      ( wetSolidCo  airCo );
        outputs     ( drySolidCo  exhaustCo );
        operation
        {
            flow              cocurrent;
            area              300 m2;        // gas-solid contact area
            k_Y               [1 -2 -1 0 0]  0.05;
            criticalMoisture  0.12;           // kg/kg dry solid
            dryingCurve
            {
                shape     power;
                exponent  1.5;
                source    "hypothetical teaching curve -- not a measured material";
            }
        }
    }
    {
        name        counter;
        type        convectiveDryer;
        inputs      ( wetSolidCounter  airCounter );
        outputs     ( drySolidCounter  exhaustCounter );
        operation
        {
            flow              countercurrent;
            area              300 m2;        // gas-solid contact area
            k_Y               [1 -2 -1 0 0]  0.05;
            criticalMoisture  0.12;           // kg/kg dry solid
            dryingCurve
            {
                shape     power;
                exponent  1.5;
                source    "hypothetical teaching curve -- not a measured material";
            }
        }
    }
);
